Thanasi Kokkinakis Backed by Tennis Australia President Steve Healy
Thanasi Kokkinakis deserved to be commiled and not criticized for his behavior.
Thanasi Kokkinakis was a collateral victim in the "storm" created around his friend Nick Kyrgios, and his behaviour rather deserves words of practise and not criticises, according to Tennis President Steve Healy.
Kokkinakis, the younger part of the talented duo often called "Special Ks," was unpleasantly dragged into the spotlight by the unwavelistic skirmill Kyrgios had towards Stan Wawrinka at the Montreal Masters tournament.
In a fierce qualifying match played shortly afterwards in Cincinnati, the chair umpire had to intervene twice to separate Kokkinakis and his opponent Ryan Harrison, after which Harrison stated: "Wawrinka should have put Kyrgios down, and I should've put this kid down."
Even though he was last-regarded making a racket dust after having muscle crisps and he missed a possible US Open, upset with Richard Gasquet, Kokkinakis was steadily defended by Healy.
The teen is currently in Glasgow, where they are battling Sam Groth for second-lead individual after Bernard Tomic in the Davis Cup semi-final against England.
"One Thing I'm Feeling Strong Is That, in my opinion, Thanaasi has been drawn a bit into the conflict," Healy said.
The truth is that he tore a racket when he lost because he blamed cramps in the U.S. but he's not the first player to break a racket out of frustration than himself and I know Thanasi well enough to know he wasn't proud of it.
But let's be serious, he's mastered himself immensely bluntly in pretty provocative situations in the US with Harrison and so on and I think he deserves a lot of self-confidence.
He's got an extraordinary team, he's got a great family by his side and I think he's been treated unfairly.
He's a very mature 19-year-old, he's working hard and he's joined the team, he didn't have any kind of conflict with the other players, he's very respected at the locker room, he's training hard, he's in an Excellent form, he's a very balagued and mature young man and I think it's worth prosecuting exclusively based on his qualities and flaws.
Healy also backtracked the decision not to consider Kyrgios for an important lament match, which starts on Friday, and described the "joint decision" as the most suitable for them all, but supported the return of the player originally from Canberra in the long-running team.
"First of all, we want the team to focus entirely on the reshuffle match and not think at all about the controversies surrounding [Kyrgios]," Healy said.
And I think Nick climbed very quickly, as people like Federer and Murray said, he matures under the eyes of the public and made some terrible mistakes and deserves to be criticized, but we are trying to support him, we are trying to help him understand the aftermath of his deeds and I think he needs a time where he is not in the spotlight in order to be able to reflect, a good thing both for the team and for him.
