Smoothing on-the-StammEC (intervene) recorded the smallest increase in August and the culprit could be Black Friday
In the first three months of 2015, online sales increased by 7%.
In the last month there has been the slowest-blown growth since 2000.
Online sales gained momentum three years ago, due to the use of the mobile phone to shop.
Specialists are of the opinion that Black Friday in November could cause reluctance at spending.
For more than over a decade, online groceries have exploded and have turned commercial streets in the UK into phantom cities.
However, experts say there are signs that online sales are shrinking, the latest figures pointing an increase of only 7% in the first three months of 2015.
The total amount of money Spenched in August showed the smallest increase since 2000, with an increase of just 5 per cent over the past year.
Specialists say there are signs that online sales are shrinking, the latest figures pointing to an increase of only 7% in the first three months of 2015.
"Would we look at the level of growth in previous years and go back by 2000, there has always been a tendency to rationalise over time," Andy Mulcahy, an IMRG sales fidlmier, told the Guardian.
He explained that online sales gained momentum three years ago when shoppers started using their mobile phone to shop.
Thus, it increased the number of people who were able to shop online and be able to do so in various circumstances, such as from the couch.
But now that they are stagnating a little, you may notice a decrease (of the increase).
The Black-Diventure phenomenon, imported from America, is mentioned as one of the reasons for declining sales.
In the opinion of the specialists, the shoppers would abstain from making purchases before the event that takes place in the last Friday in November.
Last year, five times the average number of shoppers crippled in front of their computers to get their hands on the offerings, which led to the collapse of the websites of several retailers.
Specialists are of the opinion that Black Friday, when shoppers got drunk on bargains, could determine the reluctance of shoppers.
