Advantages and Disadvantaugic Conversation of Loans in Swiss Francs
On the banking market there have already appeared two offers to convert loans into Swiss francs in lei, the main advantages being excluding foreign exchange risks and reducing rates.
Disadvantautizations can come in a few years, when variable interest rates can lead to increased rates.
Banca Transilvania has just completed the process of conversing loans into francs granted by Volksbank, and Bancpost has announced the conversion offer for its own customers.
According to data cumulated by Analysts of FinZoom financial portal, Bancpost offers three fixed-interest years at M(s, of 1.5%, the lowest on the market.
On the other hand, Banca Transilvania’s conversion offer was accepted by 83% of the clients with loans in francs due to the second successive reductions: the balance was reduced by the value of the risk and administration fees paid by the client, then an additional reduction of 22.5% was applied to the balance in lei.
Both offers reduce the installments of their own customers, compared to how much they paid monthly equivalent in lei before the conversion.
Thus, Banca Transilvania reduces the rate by 42.5%, and Bancpost, by 33% in the first three years and, theoretically, by 18% in the coming years.
Benefit
The main advantage of pretsiege in lei of loans in CHF is that the foreign exchange risk is eliminated, the consumer being protected by the future increases of the franc, and the repayment of the loan will be made in the currency in which he collects his salary.
Another major advantage of both conversion offerings is that property re-assesssessment is not required, additional guarantees are no longer required and another proof of income.
Practically, a new financial and legal analysis of the credit will not be carried out and there will not be any additional costs in the case of a refinance.
Also, both banks offer discounts at the monthly rate, compared to how much the customers paid before the conversion, equivalent in lei.
Risks
The problem is that the duck in the early years consists of more interest, and the customer reimburses very little from the principal.
Bancpost's offer with a very low interest rate in the first three years seems advantageous for the customers.
It is not known how this interest will evolve after three years.
The margin offered to customers can be more than 3.25 per cent, so, on the total, and the cost-smoke reduction is lower.
Possible risk for all variable-interest loans in lei, the ROBOR index increases significantly in the coming years (more than the Libor - CHF index).
For example ROBOR every three months is now 1.44%, but in 2013 it reached 6%, in 2009 over 15%, and in a few days of 2008 it exceeded 40%!
In addition, it is also possible to defend other costs, not mentioned in the initial offer.
