The French violinist David Grimal is one of the great artists of our time, known not only as a soloist but also as the initiator of a less common project, downright “weird”, according to him, Les Dissonances – an orchestra consisting of international soloists and talented musicians, who perform a symphonic repertoire without having a conductor, and this makes it at least a curiosity.
Les Dissonances was this year at the Enescu Festival with two performances: a performance, on the Sunday, in which David Grimal, the founder of the orchestra, and one on Monday, when in the foreground was cellist Xavier Phillips, the works compiled by Enescu being unbimissable.
Both performances, held on the Athenaeum stage, showed the audience a different kind to perform classical music.
Les Dissonances appeared on the music scene in 2004 being, at that time, the only classical musical ensemble in which there was no conductor.
The chariot is the most important musician for the musicians.
It's true, on the one hand the musicians need an education either to become soloists or to become performers in an orchestra - I'm talking about instrumentalists, and I don't like composers.
They're formed like "specialized workers."
They have the impression that they need a conductor.
On the other side of the stage, for the audience, all musical fetishization focuses on the conductor.
And thus, business, the music industry, is doing its marketing.
Let's no longer talk about music.
So, if we look at this world, on the one hand we have skilled workers, which is stupid, and an audience that can't listen, but it's supposed to watch, which is stupid again," said David Grimal.
The violinist revisides that this change in the way he makes the music came after the "liquital depictions" of his career as a soloist.
"I was like a high-profile athlete who was supposed to do the Same exercise all over the world with different orchestras, in order to earn a lot of money and the music industry.
10 years ago, when I was about 30, I found this very depressing, because I couldn’t find human creativity and brotherhood anymore in this system.
Therefore, I did not find any sense to continue on this road.
That's how I decided to create Les Dissonances.
This happened in 2004, and at that time no one was doing anything like this without a conductor: “The musical world, extremely conservative, didn’t want such projects.”
"How do I imagine you to do marketing with a collective of musicians where there's no ego anymore?" asks the artist.
David Grimal recognizes that Les Dissonaces represents "a luxury", specifying that none of the musicians who are part of the orchestra depends on the success of this project in order to live.
“Curiquitly, the more complexion’s lack of a conductor has not proved to be an obstacle in the musical act: “Curiquiously, the more complex we sing a repertoire, the simpler it seems, because all the musicians are obliged to be present in the opera and not just in the role”, Grimal argues, adding that at Sunday’s show “there were 74 conductors on the stage”.
In Grimal’s opinion, the Enescu Festival is “Very Brave”, precisely because he invited Les Dissonances: “An Extraordinary festival, where the largest orchestras in the world are present, which plays 3 times a day, and in the middle of these orchestras you also invited this strange group”.
In a temple of orchestral tradity, such as the Enescu Festival, there is the place and the interest for something different that breaks barriers.
Talking about Enescu’s work, the violinist admits that he feels the Romanian music of the notorious Romanian composer, so he didn’t need a special approach when he introduced works by Enescu in his repertoire.
Grimal notes that Enescu's music is very instinctive, but this is what happens when we talk about George Enescu and Romanian music, "because there is also a George Enescu who made French music".
Grimal militates for changing the way classical musical education is done, considering that "the profession of musician, as it is organized today, has a sense of death" and that the "future of music represents the music itself".
We don’t need to tour the world 5 times a year if it doesn’t serve.
In the age of Bach there were no world tours.
Beethoven, Brahms, Bartok, Enescu were people who worked, who were artists and not commercial representatives.
For me, the organization of concerts, festivals, tournaments, the release of albums, they are all formidable, extraordinary, but for as long as it is done in the service of music.
If it doesn't, we're in a system where creativity suffers, it no longer has freedom.
In order to illustrate this phenomenon, David Grimal set an example for Romanian products and post-acknowledgment to the European Union: "In Romania, before you entered the EU, you had good vegetables and meat".
In order to enter the EU you had to entourage sanitary laws that are not good for vegetables and meat.
The production and agriculture end up in the service of supermarkets, as it happens in France.
The same thing happens with the music, it’s the same logic.
Les Dissonances makes tomato maybe not too beautiful, but when we cut them there are real tomatoes.
In an all-edevelous world, this music should come back to the center and for the institutions and industries that manage this system to understand that music doesn't need to be sold, it doesn't need to be shared, and this can be done starting with educational policy.
"If we considered - as the Koreans do, the Chinese, the Japanese - that Western music is one of the most beautiful things of humanity, we would re-introduce his learning in the school, we reintroduce concerts on television, and through this education we would no longer need to sell musical by-products," Grimal said.
Definitely, there's things in classical musical education that remain very communist, that's everywhere, it's something very militarized.
It's up to this discipline whether to compare a musician with a soldier, or whether we're pounding a musician who's singing an instrument, being, first of all, a musician.
We are talking about a completely different education, if we want us to form a soldier (education) it has to be in some way, if we want to form a musician, it's something else.
In your opinion, what is more dangerous, a soldier or a musician?" asks the French artist smiling.
Grimal shows that, for the artists, the bounty of a different way of making the music comes precisely from overcoming this "soldier status", overcoming what "they think are their limits": "Musicians discover at a certain moment that they are not soldiers, and that's why after the concert they emerge smiling".
David Grimal spoke about his musical project and about his vision of music and the artist during the "Festival of Ideas", a discussion with the audience organized within the George Enescu International Festival by the Aspen Institute Romania.
Profile David Grimal
Following Conservatoire National Superieur de Musique in Paris under Regis Pasquier's ennoblement, he received advice and encouragement from renowned artists like Schllomo Mintz and Isaac Stern, David Grimal studied for one year at the ECOle des Science Politiques in Paris before meeting Philippe Hirshhorn that changed the course of his life.
"David Grimal has an international solo violinist's career, during which he has regularly held concerts for the last 20 years on the world's main classical music scenes with prestigious orchestras such as Paris Orchestras, Philharmonique Orchestres of Radio France, Russian National Orchestra, Orchestre National of Lyon, Chamber Orchestra of Europe, Berliner Symphoniker, New Japan Philharmonic, Orchestre of the
It was the recipient of some works deafeated by several composers, including Marc-Andre Dalbavie, Brice Pauset, Thierry Escaich, Jean-Franchois Zygel, Alexander Gasparov, Victor Kissine, Fuminori Tanada, Richard Dubugnon, Ivan Fedele, Philippe Harrowing, Anders Hillborg, Oscar Bianchi, Guillaume Connesson.
In addition to his career as a solo artist, David Grimal wanted to explore even more personal projects.
The freedom offered by the collaboration with the Les Dissonances allowed him to develop his inner universe by venturing into repertoires that are unaccessible to solo artists.
Together with Les Dissonances, he founded "L'Autre Saison", a series of concerts given for the benefit also with the homeless at the church of Saint-Leu, located in the heart of Paris.
Beginning in 2008, David Grimal was an Artist in Residing at Dijon Opera.
Teach ven courses at Musikhochschule in Sarrebruck, Germany.
It also holds a lot of craftsmanship courses and was a member of the Long-Thibaud International Constilary Contest in Paris in the year 2010.
He was awarded Chevalier des Arts et des Lettres from the French Minister of Culture in 2008.
David Grimal sang on a Stradivarius "ex Roederer" of 1710, but "Don Quixote", a violn designed for him by French luthier Jacques Fustier.
David Grimal was present on Sunday at the George Enescu International Event Festival this year, together with the Les Dissonances Orchestra, with Debussy, Enescu and Beethoven.
Les Dissonances also took the stage on Monday, this time with cellist Xavier Phillips in the foreground, and the program was composed of Schnittke, Enescu and Brahms.
