Rare disease that a man suffers from
U.K. Nick Hess was smuggled out by his family as an undercoactor, the wife even filming him when he was dizzy, he couldn't just stand up and his words were hard to understand.
Although the 35-year-old said he didn't drink alcohol, it was noticeable.
When she first blamed me for being drunk, without drinking, I thought she's crazy.
"But friends and family thought 'maybe he drank on his by himself, he's an alcoholic in secrecy.
"When he saw his wife's videos of him, the man in Columbus, Ohio, just didn't recognize himself: "I was staring in my eye and I wasn't there."
'It's been appalling. I couldn't remember anything.'
In addition, his wife, who, although he doubted for a while, finally went with him to countless doctors, remembers that Nick's breath also smelled of alcohol.
The moment that made Nick most thought was when, after a minor accident, when he was hit by another car, it was discovered that he had an alcoholism three times over the legal, although the night before he had consumed only a single beer.
After years and countless investigations, it has been discovered that Nick suffers from an extremely rare "Self-Brewery Syndrome".
Practically, its body causes all the carbohydrates consumed to ferment, turning into alcohol.
The one who suspected that this could be Nick's disease was even his wife.
The disease doesn't have a cure yet, but ever since he gave up carbohydrates, Nick hasn't had such episodes.
