On Wednesday, the Government endorsed "National Immigration Strategy for 2015-2018" and "Equity plan for the current year", documents considered to be "another stage that Romania is undergoing the efforts to modernize the immigration management process on the national territory", according to a press release.
The quoted source shows that the strategy is aimed at the efficient management of the immigration phenomenon, including general strategic objectives, specific objectives and directions of action that reflect the policies established at European level, adapted to the national realities.
Taking into account the problems faced by the states in the immediate vicinity of Romania, measures are taken into account for the unitary and coherent management of the situations determined by the possible emergence of an influx of illegal immigrants on the territory of Romania, generated by situations of crisis of a political, social, economic or military nature.
"The Romanian authorities will contribute to the creation of a system of flexible admission, but, at the same time, they will pay special attention to the citizens who come from the states with a perimigration potential or who can affect the national security," the source adds.
The strategy identifies a number of factors that can influence immigration and asylum, among them maintaining the conflict situation in Syria, the reactivation of the conflict in Iraq, maintaining an unsmart situation in areas bordering Romania (Ukraine, Transnistria), the perpetuation and growth of the Israeli-Palestinian armed conflict.
On the other hand, the document also fixes some general strategic objectives related to immigration, such as: the promotion of legal migration, more rigorous controls the legality of the stay of third-party citizens on the territory of Romania and the proper application of removal measures and restrictive measures, the improvement of the national asylum system.
The national strategy also includes specific objectives relevant for the transposition and implementation of general strategic objectives.
Among these are: the facilitation of access on the territory of third-party citizens who respond to the employment needs of the nationals of third-country nationals to the higher education institutions in Romania for the fields and professions identified as deficient.
Regarding the Action Plan for 2015, the Government considers that it represents "a means by which concrete activities are established to lead to the creation of a flexible system to stop the growing migration phenomenon".
Romania, in a European top regarding the labor market
Latvia, Romania and Bulgaria recorded the largest increase in the total labor cost among the member states of the European Union in the second quarter of the year, compared to the similar period of 2014, according to data published on Wednesday by the European Statistical Office (Eurostat).
Indications refer to employer personnel expenses.
Thus, between April and June 2015, the total cost of the workforce increased by 1.9% in the European Union and by 1.6 per cent in the euro area.
Romania ranks third in the EU to increase the cost by labor
The largest increases were reported in Latvia (7.9%), Romania (7.7%), Bulgaria (6.8%), Estonia (5.5%) and Lithuania (4.3%).
The only EU states in which the hourly labour cost decreased in the second trimester of this year (minus 2.9%), Cyprus (minus 1.2%) and Italy (minus 0.4%).
Advances of labor costs in Romania between April and June 2015 are mainly due to an increase in the hourly labor cost in industry and services, by 7.9 per cent and 7.5 per cent respectively.
In the construction sector, Romania registered an advance of 5.8% of the hourly cost of another workforce.
The activity report of the TVR management, rejected in committees: Stelian Tanase risked the dismissal
The cultural commissions of the Parliament rejected on Wednesday the activity report of the Board of Directors of the Romanian Television Society for 2014.
For the rejection of the report they voted 17 MPs, one against the rejection (Senourtain Varujan Vosganian) and one abstained.
The text of the report will be forwarded to the reunited Standing Bureaus.
They're going to decide, probably next week.
At the next Joint Plenary it will be debated.
"I think that in view of this situation it will be done relatively quickly," Senate Culture Committee Chairman Georgica Severin said.
He added that "it was a common sense" for the TVR management to be present at this meeting, given that at the meeting of the Culture Committees there was not present any representative of the TVR's leadership.
It was a common sense to present the TVR leadership because no one here underwent personal attacks or anything like that.
The invitation was made to CA members as early as last night, an approximate hour that we didn’t even know the hour, but today, after the meeting of the Joint Offices, I personally sent a fax to the TVR leadership inviting it.
I believe that at the moment when you are summoned, you being a budgetary employee, paid from the public taxes and you are summoned to a meeting of the joint commissions even if you don’t like it alive.
"Because it's your service obligation, you're in an institution subordinated to Parliament," Severin said after the meeting of Parliament's Joint Culture Committees.
"He added that during the meeting were discussed the financial aspects of the institution based on the negative opinion of the Budget Committees.
Debtarios we also saw how they continued, it came up with the idea that this year it would have been up-the-a-file, but they didn’t get paid from the historical ones.
"I remind you that it wasn't the fault of me or anyone else in these committees that it's that recreation with benches and the IRS, there's clear management issues," Severin said.
He added that after the activity report will be dismissed by the Joint Plenary, there will be an elections for a new Board of Directors, and he will elect the director general of the institution.
I don't know who's going to be proposed for this position.
Miracles aren't going.
It’s important to make political decisions so that this institution works.
I'm not the man to lie to you, let's just tell you that there's going to show a genius who's gonna stand in the queue here and fight.
I hope that those who decide, the plenum of the Parliament, through their vote will choose with a lot of reason the people who will lead TVR.
There must be people who know what’s going to happen there, to have the experience and to have a lot of fairness and respect for the institution and the tradition.
I don't know who it will be, if you ask me what qualities they need to have - common sense in the attitude towards the employees because it's a pile of petitions that come from employees, complaints, layoffs.
"Whether you receive such notifications 50, 60, 70, something is rotten and we are talking about the financial situation that is a disaster", concluded the Chairman of the Senate Culture Committee.
Although he didn’t present himself at the Parliament’s president-general, Stelian Tanase, sent a letter to the Speaker of Deputies, Valeriu Zgonea, in which he calls for the Parliament to envisage a series of measures that could lead to saving and guaranteeing the economic and political independence of the public television, among them being the indexation of the TV tax.
In the letter, Stelian Tanase requested, in addition to the indexation of the TV tax, amending and completing GD 978/2003 regarding the fee for the public television service, in order to update the provisions at the level of up-to-to-date technological changes, as well as for regulating the control procedure in the field of paying the TV tax and ascertaining contraventions and the application of sanctions and penalties.
Moreover, he also wants to issue a provision that will stipulate the extension of the obligation for all suppliers of electricity to become trustees of SRTv to collect the TV tax, through the involvement of ANRE in the regulation of this activity.
In his opinion, it is necessary to establish a maximum ceiling of 5 per cent of levied commission.
Stelian Tanase calls for the reduction of the VAT rate for the activities specific to the public TV station and the contribution for the CNC from 15% to the level of 3%, but also the modification of the law 41/1994, in the point of being able to receive budget/financing allowances for the acquisition of licenses for major cultural and sporting events - the World Football Championship, Golden Stag, Eurovision.
He argues that SRTv will default in the next few months given that the labor legislation obliges the employer to implement the court decisions, even if they are not final, and the salary rights are honored as a priority over any other obligation.
The TVR administration has made constant efforts to reduce the expenses of personnel and TV production.
"Thus shifts and other cost-generating activities were reduced, a number of commercial contracts were renegotiated, and at the end of the end of July TVR News it was closed," reads TVR's letter.
Tanase believes that the disastrous financial situation cannot only be remedied by its own efforts.
"Without the support of the institutions of the state - the Government, the Parliament, the Presidence - the Romanian Television will sooner or later be unable to fulfill its public mission," the letter said.
The Chamber of Deputies decided to send the letter to the Culture Committee.
Vasile Blaga's son-in-law sent to trial
Vasile Blaga’s son-in-law, Ionut Andrei Rudeanu, was prosecuted on Wednesday by the prosecutors of the Prosecutor’s Office attached to the Bucharest Court of Appeal.
He is accused of tax evasion, in a dossier with damages of about 600,000 euros, being under judicial control.
Andrei Rudeanu was remanded in custody on May 22.
He set up the guard company Axis Corporate Security SRL, together with his mother, in April 2009, a year after he married Vasile Blaga’s Daughter Agathe.
From the Investigations it turned out that, in the purpose of evading the payment of taxes to the state-of-the-state budget, the representatives of a company specialized in the provision of security services would have created a criminal circuit, consisting of several commercial companies, through which fictitious transactions would have been highlighted.
"Actually, fictitious acquisitions of services would have been registered in the company's accounting, which, in reality, were not provided, the state's budget being thus prejudiced by about 600,000 euros," the Romanian Police explained in a press release.
The Sheikhs have established Sanmartean's fate
Sheikh of Al-Ittihad has established the feat of mid-Milk Lucian Sanmartean.
Although the Arab press reports that Sanmartean's separation from ittihad is imminent, the manager of coach Ladislau Boloni, Arcadie Zaporojanu, says that this will not happen again.
Boloni is very satisfied with Sanmartean.
Lucian plays the match holder and he is doing it well.
Sheikhs also realized that he's the best and it'd be wrong if they gave him up.
It would still have been the option to bring in another player, much better, but nothing came out.
"There have been a number of discussions, such as the one with Mbark Boussoufa, but there has not materialized anything," Zaporojanu told Digi Sport.
Lucian Sanmartean arrived at Al-Ittihad earlier this year.
Ilie Sarbu voted vice-president at the Court of Auditors
The Plenum of the Parliament voted on Wednesday the appointment of PSD Senator Ilie Sarbu, Prime Minister Victor Ponta’s father-in-law, as vice-president of the Audit Authority from the Court of Accounts.
According to Mediafax, there were registered 290 votes "for" and 108 "against".
The vote was secret, with ballots.
PNL representatives were the only ones who voted against this proposal, Digi24 informs.
Moreover, the liberals criticized Sarbu’s appointment in this position and announced that they will challenge it at the CCR, given that he has a dossier at the DNA, but he does not have the necessary training and experience.
Ilie Sarbu has at his disposal 30 days, according to the law, to resign from the Senate, because the new position from the Court of Accounts does not allow him to do politics anymore.
The PSD Senator will serve as vice-chairman of the Audit Authority at the Court of Accounts, which remained free after Ioan Anton was revoked, as a result of a Constitutional Court decision from September 2014, which found that he was called unconstitutional.
Surprise from one of the Sexiest Actors: I've Grown Up in the Kitchen
As Bradley Cooper is going to play the role of a chef in "Burnt", the actor made a revelation that his fans didn't expect.
In a recent interview, he stated that he has grown more in the kitchen: "I worked most of my life as a chef".
Revealed that he has worked in the kitchen since he was the age of 15, when he was working for a Greek restaurant, and then, in college, to continue in the same way.
The actor explained that his mother has Italian origins, so his whole childhood he cooked with his Italian grandmother, reports ContactMusic.
Asked what plans for the future he had at that time, Bradley Cooper said he had never been the kind of man to plan for five years, "not at least for two."
"It's not motivating for me to have a map at all," he said.
"I've got dreams, things you'd shave if I'd tell you," he also said, stating that what he'd wanted from his early childhood had come true.
At the age of the age of 40, however, the actor has admitted that he sees himself in the near future directing, starring Clint Eastwood's model, who started directing also at this age.
Ponta Accused of Uncontrollable Slanders and Irrational Claims
Hungarian Foreign Minister Peter Szijjarto harshly condemned, on Wednesday in the morning, Romanian Prime Prime Minister Victor Ponta's claims that political decision-makers in Hungary are "nothing better than those in Syria, Libya or other countries from which the refugees are fleeing."
"Yesterday, Victor Ponta lost his self-control and, through his irrational statements, offend the entire Hustlery," the Hungarian foreign minister said in a statement.
Civilized European politicians don't say such uncontrolled slanders.
"His remarks have only one explanation: he has totally lost his support in domestic politics in Romania," the head of Hungarian diplomacy added, according to MTI.
In Tuesday evening's Facebook post, Prime Minister Victor Ponta said he wanted to attract attention and sanction the behavior of senior Hungarian government officials, who, in his opinion, "are a disgrace for the culture and values of the European Union."
I don't want to respond to the provocations coming from Hungarian government representatives.
"These are a disgrace for the culture and values of the European Union; and in anti-Semitic stances, also regarding the treatment of minors of all kinds, and now during the refugee crisis!" Ponta said on Facebook.
The Romanian Prime Minister added that he is adept at rational and sustainable solutions for the refugee crisis, appreciating that brutalities and aggressive laws will not solve the problem.
Barbecue sarma, aggressive laws, prisons and brutality certainly won’t solve the problem – they’ll just show that in Hungary, in the heart of Europe, there are political decision makers with nothing better than those in Syria, Libya or the other countries from which refugees are fleeing!
"I believe in solidarity, in sensual and sustainable solutions and especially in the superiority of European values - what we see in the last few days in Hungary is in the total contradiction precisely with these objectives and values!" the head of the Romanian executive stressed.
Earlier on Tuesday, Hungarian Foreign Minister Peter Szijjarto called as "implicit lies" Romanian Prime Prime Minister Victor Ponta's remarks regarding Hungary's management of the refugee crisis, eprecincing that the statements were unsuitable for a 21st-fifth-half-half leader, according to MTI.
The head of the Hungarian diplomacy also mentioned on Tuesday that the "extremist and false remarks" of the Romanian prime minister are related to the domestic political crisis in Romania and he specified that Budapest awaited an apology from the Romanian side.
Szijjarto was referring to a statement made on Monday by Prime Minister Victor Ponta.
What I've always said and are always saying is that I'm against xenophobic speeches the idea of shutting down the borders, that all those who're coming are terrorists, it's nonsense of the Middle Ages and I'm totally opposed to this approach.
If we can receive more, we will receive, the problem is of our logistical ability to treat those people as people, not the way our Hungarian neighbors treat them, with beating and with the insertion.
“I don’t agree with that,” the head of the Romanian executive said at a press conference in Targu-Jiu.
Also on Tuesday, Szijjarto announced that the government in Budapest has decided to start preparations in the field for the extension to the Romanian border of the fence it is building on the border with Serbia, in order to cope with an eventual change in the route used by the migrants.
Subsequently, Prime Prime Minister Victor Ponta stated that Hungary’s decision to build a fence at the borders did not solve anything about the refugee problem and “take Hungary out of Europe”.
What the connection is between Osama bin Laden and Mecca crane crash
Saudi authorities have announced that the Sautied giant Binladin Group, belonging to Osama bin Laden's family, is "partially responsible" for the collapse of a crane on the Grand Mosque in Mecca on Friday, an accident following which 107 people have died.
According to an investigative commission, the company did not "observe the safety rules", the Saudi news agency (SPA) reported.
Directors of the company were banned from leaving the country until the investigation was completed in this case.
Also, the company will be excluded from new public contracts.
The construction company is owned by the former al-Qaida terrorist leader Osama bin Laden.
Osama bin Laden's father was founded 80 years ago and is currently led by the brother of the former terrorist leader, Bakr.
Binladen Group has been working for 4 years on the expansion of Large Mosques, in order to be able to accommodate several pilgrims.
The accident happened on Friday evening, during a thunderstorm, when a crane, probably unbalanced by strong wind gusts, collapsed on the eastern side of the Grand Mosque at Mecca.
As a result of the accident, 107 people died and almost 400 were injured.
How Hagi Shusted The Mouth of Disputes
Considered by a lot of people the greatest Romanian footballer in history, Gica Hagi was labeled, after relinquisition, as a weak coach, who can not be able to achieve performance in the new position.
After unfortunate experiences at the national team, Steaua or Galatasaray, Hagi took over, a year ago, the role of coach at Viitorul Constanta, the team he patronizes.
Exactly one year after taking over the team, the King has demonstrated that he is capable of transforming a team that is struggling to save from relegation into a top-of-the-line one.
Thus, after a year, Viitorul registered, with Hagi coach, 16 wins, 11 draws and 11 defeats.
The toll is one of a top-of-the-line team that is battling at the European cups.
Threats have taken an even better turn for the Future this season, as the team reached the second place in the League 1, with 19 points, one less than leader Astra Giurgiu.
The future also has the best attack in League 1, with 20 goals (on a par with Astra), more than double the star.
On the other hand, even if he brought to the team several experienced players, Hagi has continued the policy of promoting youngsters, and in the last period in the starting team appeared names such as Ianis Hagi or Hodorogea.
The club’s policy has made the Future a team to watch for teams from abroad, so Hagi sold 1.5 million euros this summer - Ianis Hagi (Fiorentina - 1 million euros) and Alexandru Mitrita (Bars - 500 thousand euros).
Along with Steaua, Viitorul is the only profitable team in League 1, according to data from the Trade Registry.
In the refugee crisis, Romania has hitherto played as a small country.
It's a legitimate strategy, but after that you can't ask to be treated like the EU's seventh country.
The great countries come with solutions for the entire EU, says Cristian Ghinea, a member of the Board of Directors of the Romanian Center for European Policies.
In an interview with Ziare.com, Cristian Ghinea pointed out that Europe cannot be transformed into a fortress, and the purely police means will not work in this crisis: "Perhaps we have doubts about the quality of the values with which these people come".
But it's the masses that've started and we can't return history.
Migration is a reality to which we must adapt as well as possible and adapt them to us.
In Cristian Ghinea’s opinion, we are at an extremely emotional moment, in which politicians feel the need to show their muscles.
After he passes, the Europeans will not be willing to give up free movement, but the rules in the Schengen area must be changed: "Schengen is now like an animal created in another era and which was not adapted for the current situation".
Where are we in the refugee crisis after this JHA council who, in fact, didn't solve anything?
It's clear the EC president in agreement with Donald Tusk they didn't want to forcour a ballot in the council.
All the prognostications said he would have had the necessary votes.
Ahead of the Lisbon Treaty, the principle of unanimity was applied in JHA and any state could block any decision.
That's why things were very little integrated into justice and home affairs.
According to Lisbon, the normal procedure is applied, in which the EC has an initiatory, and the council approved it with the vote of 55% of the states, i.e. 16, representing 65% of the EU population.
Calculations said that the votes existed.
They didn't wanna go ahead.
Because it's a sensible matter, there's responsibiliary reactions even in Germany against Chancellor Merkel's unsponsibility policy and they didn't want in many countries to appear as an "Eweiling"-type reaction.
They adjudicated, therefore, but the problem remains because, obviously, this wave creates a huge problem for the Schengen governance and we will probably witness big changes there in the coming period.
So the obligatory quotas weren't really eliminated, but the subject was just let cool?
Yes, on the other hand, we are talking about a greater involvement of Frontex Agency in the management of the external borders, which means a change from the individual responsibility of the members who are at the external border of Schengen to the responsibility of the Community agencies.
Greece's border with Turkey is also Germany's boundary with Turkey, de facto, and this wave of migrants has laid the limits of the old Schengen system.
Perhaps the Dublin principle will waiver, according to which migrants are the responsibility of the state in which they first enter, because it is unsuccessable.
If Greece happets, you can't say indefinitely that it's Greece.
The problem is common.
Precisely because the problem is common, it is possible to solve without a precise mechanism, perhaps even mandatory, to divide the effort of reception?
No one knows exactly how the mandatory quotas will work, if it comes to them.
Because there remains a problem: what do you do with a man who doesn’t want to come to Romania or to Portugal, but to Sweden or Germany?
I don’t think the European states are going to go upstairs to the migrants: you’re going to country X or the Y country.
It will probably come to some unique filtration centers, in Greece or in Turkey, where the asylum applications will be processed.
They're working on solutions like that.
But the problem doesn't have a simple solution.
There are countries that have tens of years of experience in trying to integrate migrants, there are unexperienced countries like Romania, where the migrant problem will increase the xenphobic current.
It is certain that we are talking about millions of people who are in Turkey, in Lebanon, in Jordan, who are sitting in precarious camps and who have only just started to leave for Europe.
An estimation says that only 10 per cent got to go away, in winter it's hard to cross the Mediterranean, but we're expecting next year a wave maybe even bigger.
What do we do with these people?
My prognosis is that we will get used to this problem, we won't look so catastrophically from a media point of view.
You're talking about countries with less or greater experience.
Do you think there’s a place where it’s really succeeded in integrating large groups from an extra-Greek culture?
I'm staring at Maghrebians in France, the Turks in Germany, they're also enslaved.
I wouldn't go for radical statements.
Of course, their integration is more difficult than other waves of migration, but that doesn't mean they don't integrate at all.
It remains an unintegrated part that creates major social problems in France, in Germany, in the United Kingdom.
But purely police means won't work.
Creating the Fortress Europe is illusory.
The mechanisms of integrating into European societies must be tampered with.
There's been some mistakes here.
We have to be more pretentious.
Let's ask these people to learn the language, to inquire to take over the values, to no longer stay with one leg in Europe and with one in their country of origin, bringing the rest of their family even through convenience marriages.
The impembending problem of these people must be separated from the long-term problem of their integration.
Failed in long-term integration doesn't mean we have to look at them dripping in the Mediterranean.
Maybe we have any doubts about the quality of the values with which these people come.
And if there's going to be an intervention in Syria, which I don't think, it doesn't mean it's going to refurbish the country overnight and you can bring them back.
Neiting solutions, we see fortresses within Europe.
Boundaries have been closed, walls are erected, border controls are being reintroduced within the Schengen area.
Is the European project in jeopardy?
The European project as such does not, the Schengen area as it was built, yes.
It's been built quite minimally and arranging the rules so that the pressure was too high on the countries where I'm setting foot for the first time these migrants.
That's what we're circulating freely around, but everyone's with their problems.
In the face of such a vein it is not possible, because some of the countries do not cope.
“When there’s such waves of emotion, the politicians want to look like they’re doing something, they don’t change much in the long run.
According to the attacks in France we do not have much more draconian anti-terrorism legislation in the EU than we had last year.
It's a vein of public emotion settling in both senses.
And she's so institutable from one week to the next that you can't make decisions based on it.
We have a lot of speech, many bellicose statements from politicians at home under the pressure of being virile.
And, then, you have the dramatic case of the drowned child in Turkey and the public opinion gets thrilled.
After the public emotion passes, we will go back to the reasons why we have created this space, this freedom of movement that is not only for the people, but also for the goods.
That's the real European single market.
And I don't think the Europeans are going to give up this principle of freedom of movement.
That there will be other rules is something else.
S.Slagen is now like an animal created in another era that was not adapted to the current situation.
How has Romania played so far in this crisis?
As a small country, which believes that, if it pulls the sap on your eyes and clogs its ears, the theme disappears.
In the face of the reality of the waves of migrants, you can choose to be part of the solution or say that it’s not your problem with a small country.
That's what I've done, it's a legitimate strategy, but after that you can't ask to be treated like the 7th EU country.
You aren't, you don't't play at big country level.
The big titles come up with solutions for the entire EU, good or bad, we are not discussing their quality now.
Just like Poland did.
On the other hand, the effect of this crisis is that Romania’s appetite for Schengen will decrease a lot.
President Iohannis says it’s maybe good that we haven’t already entered.
So we're switching from despondential entry to a more pragmatic stance, but the goal remains.
Of course, there is a risk if Romania and Bulgaria come to Schengen for the migrants to see us as an alternative route.
This does not mean that there is a reason not to enter Schengen, but to be more pragmatic.
Romania will be part of Schengen, and Schengen will exist, but transformed in the sense that external borders will become a common responsibilities, there will be unique rules of acceptance of migrants, unique deportation rules for those who do not receive asylum, unique rules for countries considered safe.
