"They're in stable condition, as the doctors say.
EXCLUSIUSIVE: An encounter that breaks your heart.
At the age of 20 he received a courageous gift: biological parents
Lost in the hospital unflatteringly, in the woes of the 90’s, when she had only two months of her life, little girl, sick and helpless, and then adopted by a wealthy family from Italy, in the same years in which the adoption was vaguely regulated, a young woman from Iasi, now a blonde and beautiful girl, was reunited together in Iasi with her parents.
Crina will turn 20 these days, and her adoptive parents from Palermo have thought to give their beloved daughter an astonishing and courageous gift: a visit to Romania, in Iasi, in order to get to get to know their fuzzy past.
"Ziarul de Iași", who witnessed the efforts to search the home family after some extremely vague indices, witnessed these meetings that practically break your heart.
Relating of reporters, the emotions of the moments, the statements of those involved, their reactions are quite moving and coercive.
Crina's story starts 20 years ago in the town of Ruginoasa, when she was only two months old.
At that time, due to a health problem, the girl had to be admitted to the hospital in the city.
There began to be misunderstood between the parents at home, and the mother was forced to forsaving her domicile.
He's decided to keep the girl and send me away from home.
He stopped the girl, and I haven't known anything since.
"When I found out that she was in the hospital, it was too late," recalled Alina, the girl's biological mother.
Crina was hospitalized together with her grandmother in Pașcani Municipal Hospital, and at that time the girl's father, Costică Balcan, went to work in the city of Alexandria, Teleorman County.
Their lives would break apart forever.
They couldn't find Crina at the hospital
After the admission, Crina's father went to work in another locality and returned after six months.
In the meantime, the girl was transferred to the hospital in Iasi, and the father, when she came back, did not find her.
I think it's been almost nine months since the girl was in the hospital.
"He may have thought he's abandoned," a granddaughter resonates.
Due to the financial problems, the grandmother was not able to come with Crina to Iasi, and the girl remained in the care of the doctors.
Because no one inquired about the fate of the child during this time, it was taken over by the General Directorate of Social Assistance and Child Welfare in Iasi, which then cared for the Placement Center in Bucium.
There have been years in which abandonment and adoption were more easily regulated compared to today in Romania.
At the Bucium Center she was found by the Italian Salvadore and by his wife, a couple from Palermo who wanted to have their child but did not succeed.
They were instantly in love with blonde Crina, and after some time she would move permanently with them to Italy.
The little girl had already done for almost two years, as long as the adoption formalities have lasted.
When I saw Crina, I felt that she was our daughter.
"As if my wife had given her a life," Salvadore, the foster father, tells us by smiling, while catching the girl's filthy wield.
In the country, Crina's Parents, Alina and Costică, continued separately the searches, each where they could.
The father asked the authorities in Ruginoasa, but did not receive an answer where his child might be.
The police then told them that it is possible to be deceased, but in Iasi the parents found out what really happened to their daughter.
When I searched for her here, they told me at the court that she was going to Italy in Palermo.
We haven’t had conditions to look for her.
We've been praying to God that it'll be okay.
I've been thinking about it in all the years that's passed, but there hasn't been any possibilities to walk, to find it.
"It's a hard one," Renina's Biological mother tells us with tears in her eyes.
However, even if the years have passed, the parents from Ruginoasa have not been forgotten.
In Italy, Salvadore and his wife explained to the girl where she came from and that her past connects her to Romania.
I was little when I found out.
I think I'm five or six years old.
I reacted as a child, I was curious.
"Would I want to get to know my parents in Romania," Crina confesses.
Her dream was going to come true.
In the month of March this year, Chief Commissioner Romică Ichim, the director of the Regional Center for Prevention, Evaluation and Drug Advising Iasi, was contacted by an acquaintance from Italy, who explained Salvadore’s desire to find Crina’s biological family.
And the search only started from a name.
After the request we had from our partners in Italy, we started the first steps.
I received only a name, Balcan Costică, then the locality, Ruginoasa.
In a short time I had an activity with the town hall there, and I could ask if there is in the locality the name I received.
That's when they gave me a few indications, it's just that the person I found wasn't the one wanted.
"It lasted almost a month before we found the girl's biological father," Chief Commissioner Romica Imchim explains.
In a total, more than three months they took the authorities to find Costica and Alina, especially since they were residially residing in another locality.
We started the steps somewhere in the month of March, and in June-July we finalized the identification of the biological parents and the discussions.
"That's when a first-meeting will take place in September," Romică Ichim added.
In the last weekend, Alina and Costică saw for the for the first time the lost girl in the hallways of the hospital in Iasi, 20 years ago.
The father, excited, could barely contain his supplications, and his hands trembled unceasingly.
He's just changing a few words with those around him.
A week before the meeting he couldn't really sleep anymore.
He was still excited, shaking.
"In order to see your child after so many years, it's something," a granddaughter tells us, who came with Costica from Ruginoasa.
Crina's Entournment with Mother and Biological Shridle
In the discussions with his daughter, who is going to study at a well-rated university in Italy, Costică Balcan told him, among other things, that if he once wants to go back to him, in the village, to come quietly: he can give him some sheep, goats, to find a boy from the village and create a household.
My mom's excited too, she cries, and she hardly thinks something like this can happen to her.
I'm very excited. I'm very excited.
I didn't think something like this could happen.
I've seen such cases on TV, I've been thinking during these years, I've hoped, but I didn't think I could be like that too.
It's very big emotions.
If he could forgive me.
I'm just hoping we'll visit in the future.
"I hope we will be able to maintain a connection," Alina's daughter-in-grass-grabbeath-grass tells us with great tears, while holding a sherb.
In him hides his tears, from happiness, because soon he will see her face after so much of a long time.
On the other hand, Crina, who will turn in only a few days, this month, 20 years, tries to reassure himself like an emotive child.
She doesn't know what to look forward to, but she's outlining a vague idea about her biological parents.
He thinks they're young, but now before the meeting they're trying to gather their thoughts, 'cause she has a lot to be said, but nothing to reproach.
"My parents are two mothers and two dads.
But my parents are, after all, those who raised me.
It's going to be a beautiful meeting, a wonderful experience.
"I hope to have a beautiful memory with them, because now there are so many things to be said," Crina unmarried.
The first family reunion in Iasi
In Unirii’s Square, under the sky of the beginning of the autumn, the family from Ruginoasa unites again its destiny with their Daughter from Palermo.
Crina is now 20 years old, she finishes the high school, according to the Italian curriculum, but she can start a new chapter with Alina and Costica, her biological parents.
It no longer separates its borders or countries, but only a barrier: that of the language.
Above which, with the help of a translator, an Italian local owner in the center of Ias, they passed easily.
Those who have been able to make this meeting possible say that it is the first event of its kind made, and that in the future, if the opportunity arises, they will do it again.
It's the first event of its kind organized, of such an emotional load, and I'm glad it's successful.
We, at the Center, have different activities, and most of the time we are associated as being an institution that deals only with consumers, people addicted to drugs.
But we're just trying to restore companies something good back.
"Wouldn't still be requested for such an event, I'll do it with great fondness," says Romica Ichim, director of the Regional Center for Prevention, Evaluation and Drug Advising Iasi.
Behind him, the Balcan family, with Crina in the middle, departs reluctant.
I'm understandable: they have a lot to say to each other.
Armistice with guns at his foot, yesterday, at PSD.
Everyone catches their soul
The camps in the Iasi branch of the PSD have concluded an armistice with a gun at their feet.
After successive rounds of discussions, held from Friday to Sunday, the first being the most tense, the PSD leaders agreed to temporarily suspend hostilities.
Presided by the executive chairman of the party, Liviu Dragnea to present a united stance, the Social-Democrats of Iasi will send him a whole list of potential candidates for positions in the party.
Late from over, the internal war in the party seems to have taken only a break to allow the combatants to draw their breath.
Dragnea didn't give an ultimatum.
He launched a very simple and very topical message in the current conditions: you try to find a solution so as not to jeopardize the party.
On October 3 we have an election in the party.
All the functions will be made available to those who want them.
"Until then, we remain in the existing structure", said the interim mayor, Mihai Chirica.
The two days of discussion at the weekend led only to the conclusion that nothing for sure had been established.
In the elections in October, a fierce competition is awaited for the position of president of the county branch of the PSD.
On the short list of the possible candidates for the main position in the Iasi Branch of the party are State Secretary Maricel Popa, Senator Florin Constantinescu, Senator Sorin Lazăr, county councilor Vasile Catea and the interim president of the County Council, Victorel Lupu.
The mayor of Schitu Duca, Mihai Mihalache, could also be added.
A variant taken into account is the candidacy of the leader of the county advisers of the PSD, Cristian Stanciu, if Lupu will no longer join the race, party sources stating that he/she is not interested in the position.
The only name that has not been mentioned by any of the participants in the negotiations in recent days is that of the former leader of the branch, Mayor Gheorghe Nichita.
The list of candidates is open.
According to the statutes, even on the day of the elections candidacies can be tabled, and it wouldn't surprise anyone if it happened.
"The important thing is that we remain united, and we will also remain after October 3, when we will have a new Executive Office, a new County Standing Office and all the new leadership structures," Cate said.
Until the organization of the internal elections, the leadership of the county branch of PSD will be ensured by Victorel Lupu, a close associate of Nichita and Stanciu.
However, Lupu is on vacation until September 29, the preparation of the elections will practically be ensured by the executive secretary of the branch, county councilor Mihail Popescu, in cold relations with the Nichita camp.
Contacted by phone, Cristian Stanciu appreciated the discussions of the last few days as leading to "a partial consensus".
There's a wide readiness to maintain equilibrium and unity in the party.
A partial consensus was reached, in the sense of supporting a unitary team to deal with the county.
"However, on the other hand, there are several variants regarding the intentions related to the candidacy for the PSD Iași presidency, the list remaining open," Stanciu commented.
Wit-Jatch refuses Vaslui violators' case because of cohabitation
The seven young people from Vaslui county convicted of rape arrive today at the Iasi Court of Appeal.
Vasluians Sentencilly Sentenced to Sentences Between Five Years and Eight Months and Eight-Months will today present their arguments regarding their release, after the substantial court, the Vaslui Court, established their incarceration immediately after the sentencing.
The dossier was registered with the Court of Appeal at the end of the last week, the magistrate Dan Anton formulating a request for abstention, admitted by his colleagues.
